  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Purushottamdas vs The State Of Madhya Pradesh on 21 February, 2024 Author: Hirdesh Bench: Hirdesh --1-- In The High Court Of Madhya Pradesh At I N D O R E Before Hon'Ble Shri Justice Hirdesh On The 21st Of February, 2024 Misc. Appeal No. 684 of 2024 Between:- 1. Purushottamdas S/O Gopaldas Gupta, Aged About 72 Years, Firm: M/S Giriraj Traders, Kalika Mall, Opposite Bhoj Kanya School, Dhar Tahsil Dhar, Distt. Dhar (Madhya Pradesh) 2. Ashok Kumar Boss Firm Shalimar Chemical Works Private Limited Not Mention (Madhya Pradesh) 3. Somnath Bhattacharya S/O P.N. Bhattacharya, Aged About 65 Years, M.P. , Firm Shalimar Chemical Works Private Limited (Madhya Pradesh) 4. Loknath Bhattacharya P.N. Bhattacharya Dead Through Lrs. Shri Kushal Bhattacharya Firm Shalimar Chemical Works Private Limited (Madhya Pradesh) 5. Chandralekha Bhattacharya S/O Shosodhar Banarjee, Aged About 55 Years, M.P. Firm Shalimar Chemical Works Private Limited (Madhya Pradesh) 6. Indrani Bhattacharya S/O Loknath Bhattacharya, Aged About 66 Years, Director, Firm Shalimar Chemical Works Private Limited (Madhya Pradesh) 7. Kushal Bhattacharya S/O Loknath, Aged About 41 Years, Whole Time Director, Firm Shalimar Chemical Works Private Limited (Madhya Pradesh) 8. Trilok Bhattacharya S/O Somnath, Aged About 33 Years, Whole Time Director, Firm Shalimar Chemical Works Private Limited (Madhya Pradesh)",
    "facts": "This petition has been filed by the petitioner under Section 104 r/w Order 43 Rule 1 of CPC and Section 151 of CPC against the order dated 26.10.2023 passed by Principal District Judge/Presiding Judge, Food Safety Appellate Tribunal, Dhar, District - Dhar in MJC No.16/2023 whereby the petitioner's application for restoration of appeal, which was dismissed on 17.02.2023, has been rejected. 2. In brief, the facts of the case are that the petitioner is engaged in the business of trading grocery items and on 06.07.2018 certain samples were obtained from firm and were sent to Food Laboratory, Bhopal where the articles were found to be mislabeled. Subsequently, final order has also been passed against the petitioner on 09.08.2021 imposing a penalty of Rs.3 Lacs on appellant No.1 and penalty of Rs.5 Lacs jointly on respondent Nos.2 & 3 and penalty of Rs.5 Lacs jointly on appellant Nos.4 to 12 and that order has been challenged by the petitioner in an appeal under Section 3.3.1 of Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 before the designated Court, Principal District --3-- Judge/Presiding Judge, Food Safety Appellate Tribunal, Dhar, and when the matter was fixed before the said Court on 17.02.2023, since on the said date the Advocates were abstaining from work on account of call made by the M.P. State Bar Council, the appeal was dismissed as the appellant was also not present before the Court. Subsequently, an application for setting aside the ex- parte order under Order 9 Rule 4 of CPC has also been rejected on the same ground.",
    "arguments": "3. Counsel has submitted that the appeal ought to have been restored because the counsel for the appellant did not appear before the Appellate Court, appellant cannot be punished for his conduct, and he was also not informed by his counsel to appear before the Appellate Court.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "3. Counsel has submitted that the appeal ought to have been restored because the counsel for the appellant did not appear before the Appellate Court, appellant cannot be punished for his conduct, and he was also not informed by his counsel to appear before the Appellate Court.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
